   "title": "Chapter 2: Radix Conversions & Power-of-2 Highway Groupings",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.1 Universal Radix Conversion Architecture"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Conversions between arbitrary radices follow the three canonical paths:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "start": 1,
     "items": [
      "**Base-$r \\to$ Decimal ($r \\to 10$):** Direct Cauchy-Laurent expansion.",
      "**Decimal $\\to$ Base-$r$ ($10 \\to r$):** Successive division (integer) and successive multiplication (fraction).",
      "**Power-of-2 Highway ($2 \\leftrightarrow 2^k$):** Direct bit-clustering without passing through decimal."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "text": "                       +-----------------------+\n                       |      Decimal (10)     |\n                       +-----------------------+\n                        ^  /                 \\  ^\n       Polynomial Sum  /  / Div/Mult   Div/Mult\\  \\ Polynomial Sum\n                      /  v                     v  \\\n         +---------------+                     +--------------------+\n         |   Base r_1    |                     |      Base r_2      |\n         +---------------+                     +--------------------+\n                 \\                                     /\n                  \\====== Power-of-2 Highway =========/\n                         (When r_1 = 2^a, r_2 = 2^b)"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.2 Base-$r$ to Decimal Evaluation"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "For any base-$r$ number, evaluate:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Val}_{10} = \\sum_{i=-m}^{n-1} d_i r^i"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Example:** Convert $(2A.8)_{16}$ to decimal:"
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "\\text{Val}_{10} = 2 \\times 16^1 + 10 \\times 16^0 + 8 \\times 16^{-1} = 32 + 10 + 0.5 = 42.5_{10}"
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.3 Decimal to Base-$r$ Algorithmic Engines"
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "start": 1,
     "items": [
      "**Integer Part \u2014 Successive Division:**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Divide the decimal integer by $r$ repeatedly. Record remainders at each step until the quotient becomes $0$.\n   The remainders are read in reverse order: from bottom to top ($\\text{LSD} \\to \\text{MSD}$)."
    },
    {
     "t": "ol",
     "start": 2,
     "items": [
      "**Fractional Part \u2014 Successive Multiplication:**"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "Multiply the fractional part by $r$. The generated integer part forms the next digit. Repeat with the remaining fraction.\n   The generated integer digits are read in forward order: from top to bottom ($\\text{MSD} \\to \\text{LSD}$)."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.4 Terminating vs. Non-Terminating (Recurring) Radix Fractions"
    },
    {
     "t": "alert",
     "type": "IMPORTANT",
     "title": null,
     "text": "**The Prime Factorization Terminating Fraction Theorem:**\nA rational fraction in irreducible form $\\frac{p}{q}$ ($\\gcd(p, q) = 1$) terminates in a positional number system of base $r$ if and only if **every prime factor** of the denominator $q$ divides the radix $r$:\n$$\\frac{p}{q} \\text{ terminates in base } r \\iff \\forall \\text{ prime } p_j \\mid q \\implies p_j \\mid r$$"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Implications:**"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "In decimal ($r = 10 = 2 \\times 5$), $\\frac{1}{10} = 0.1_{10}$ terminates because its prime factors are $\\{2, 5\\} \\subseteq \\{2, 5\\}$.",
      "In binary ($r = 2$), the prime factors of the radix are strictly $\\{2\\}$. Since $5 \\nmid 2$, the decimal fraction $0.1_{10} = \\frac{1}{10}$ **never terminates** in binary:"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "math",
     "tex": "(0.1)_{10} = (0.0001100110011\\dots)_2 = (0.0\\overline{0011})_2"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "This fundamental property causes unavoidable round-off errors in floating-point arithmetic."
    },
    {
     "t": "h3",
     "text": "2.5 Direct Power-of-2 Highway Conversions ($r = 2^k$)"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "When converting between binary and a base $r = 2^k$, each digit in base $2^k$ maps directly and independently to an isolated group of $k$ binary bits:"
    },
    {
     "t": "ul",
     "items": [
      "**Binary $\\leftrightarrow$ Octal ($k = 3$):** Group bits in clusters of 3, starting from the radix point:",
      "For integers: group leftward, padding leading zeros if needed.",
      "For fractions: group rightward, padding trailing zeros if needed.",
      "**Binary $\\leftrightarrow$ Hexadecimal ($k = 4$):** Group bits in clusters of 4, starting from the radix point.",
      "**Octal $\\leftrightarrow$ Hexadecimal:** Bridge directly via binary groups of 3 and 4 bits without decimal conversion."
     ]
    }
   ]
